<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter Parler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1333–1399</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter Parler was a German architect and sculptor, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-parler">Heinrich Parler</a>, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a>'s master builder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> from 1356. He continued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral">St. Vitus Cathedral</a> with innovative net vaulting, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-bridge">Charles Bridge</a> and its Old Town tower, and carved portrait busts in the cathedral's triforium. His workshop influenced late Gothic architecture across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-parler.jpg" alt="Peter Parler" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Packare · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busta_Petr_Parl%C3%A9%C5%99.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
